<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Fifth Coalition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Fifth Coalition was fought in 1809 between Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its allies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Austria invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and won at Aspern-Essling in May, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s first major personal defeat, but lost at Wagram in July; by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> it gave up large territories, including its Adriatic coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition.jpg" alt="War of the Fifth Coalition" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruedi33a · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_5th_coalition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had been defeated in 1805 and spent three years reforming its army. Encouraged by events in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where much of the French army was committed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>, it sought a new confrontation to avenge its defeats and regain lost territory. Its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at Tilsit, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-erfurt">Congress of Erfurt</a> in 1808 agreed to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> if Austria attacked. Austria built the largest army in its history, conscripted from across the empire, but its quality was hampered by incomplete training and the army's multinational nature. The Austrians hoped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> would join them, but it stayed neutral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 10 April 1809 Austrian forces under Archduke Charles crossed the Inn into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, a French client state, without a formal declaration of war. The attack came about a week before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> expected it, and the first French response under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</a> was disorganised until Napoleon arrived on 17 April. After fighting around Regensburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, Charles retreated eastwards but failed to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> before the French. A French crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> was repulsed on 21–22 May at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aspern-essling">Battle of Aspern-Essling</a>. After more than six weeks of preparation the French crossed again, and on 5–6 July Napoleon won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a>, a close battle in which the Austrians withdrew in good order.</p><p>Elsewhere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-john-of-austria">Archduke John</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugene-de-beauharnais">Eugène de Beauharnais</a> at Caldiero on 30 April but was beaten at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a> on 8 May and driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Austrians invaded the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, and in July a British force of over 39,000 landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walcheren">Walcheren</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the largest British expedition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a>, signed on 14 October 1809, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> and the Adriatic ports from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, ending its access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. West Galicia went to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the district of Tarnopol to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Austria lost over three million subjects, about 20 per cent of its population, and agreed to pay an indemnity of almost 85 million francs. After the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> married Marie Louise, the daughter of Emperor Francis.</p><p>Aspern-Essling showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> could be beaten on the battlefield. The war also saw risings against French rule, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-hofer">Andreas Hofer</a>'s rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, the Gottscheer rebellion and revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, which were suppressed but foreshadowed later nationalist risings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Fifth Coalition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
